I've characterized both variants of `osu_allreduce`. Summary:

**osu_allreduce / cpu (default build)**: Pure MPI collective microbenchmark. Uses `mpirun_rsh`/`mpirun` with ≥2 ranks, no threads/GPU. Tight loop of `MPI_Allreduce` + `MPI_Barrier` over increasing message sizes with no computation between calls — communication model is collective with `{all_to_all, reduction, broadcast}` patterns, high communication intensity, latency- and bandwidth-sensitive. No shared memory, no accelerator, no checkpointing. Bound by network communication with very low arithmetic intensity (single MPI_SUM reduction op).

**osu_allreduce / cuda (built with `--enable-cuda`)**: Same MPI collective loop but operating on GPU device buffers (`accel_type CUDA/MANAGED`), allocated via `cudaMalloc`-style paths in `osu_util_mpi.c`. `MPI_Allreduce` is called directly on device pointers, relying on the underlying MPI's CUDA-awareness (`MV2_USE_CUDA=1` shown in docs) rather than NCCL — so `accelerator.gpu_comm=gpu_aware_mpi`, not nccl. Single GPU per rank, no explicit multi-device handling in this file.

Both traces were recorded with evidence citations from `c/mpi/collective/blocking/osu_allreduce.c`, `c/util/osu_util.c`/`osu_util_mpi.c`, `configure.ac`, and `README.md`.
